# Magnetic Tape and Cassette
#
# Sound as magnetized particles: wire, the Magnetophon, AC bias, the Ampex studio, the compact cassette and its cultures, the Walkman, and digital audio tape.

#>media_carriers_tape
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!media_carriers
#:form #!timeline
#:level #!intro
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bonk
#:era #!modern

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Magnetic Tape and Cassette"
 ~#claim: "Tape made recording erasable and editable, and the cassette made copying cheap enough that listeners and small labels could publish."
 ~#proof: "Daniel, Mee, and Clark for the technical history; Manuel and Sreberny-Mohammadi for cassette cultures in India and Iran."
 ~#door: "Read the decay ledger for sticky tape, then the dating key to read a cassette shell."
 ~#audience: "readers with old tapes at home or an interest in how music spread before files"
 continuity: "Magnetic Tape and Cassette | copying cheap"
 cold_open: "Tape made sound erasable and editable, and the cassette made copying cheap enough for anyone to publish."
}

